+# LCCC inline-asm / Linux 6.18.58 session — 2026-10-30
+
+## Rebase and saved state
+
+- Rebased the two inline-asm commits onto the then-current `origin/main` at
+  `1c2feff17ccf8df00ac76559d9fcf9e65b96f2d7` (the remote had advanced six
+  commits from the previous base `0ff1d3f12a75ce83f491088f0ec27526f03d6db9`).
+- Git reported a clean rebase with no conflicts. Current source HEAD before this
+  document: `3e2673b0ee5f4ae2ac336bc740909b7fd95dac78`.
+- The deliverable patch is `/home/user/ms178-1.patch`; the session patch base is
+  recorded in `/home/user/artifacts/.base_ref`. The patch was verified with
+  `git apply --check` after the rebase. The current patch and series snapshots
+  are under `/home/user/artifacts/`.
+
+## Compiler changes and focused validation
+
+The initial Linux failure was an immediate-only inline-asm constraint checked
+before GCC-style constant propagation/inlining. The follow-up exposed two
+additional shapes: `__builtin_constant_p`/`__builtin_clz*` constant trees, and
+local `__auto_type __flags` values in x86 `WARN`/BUG macros. Linux also uses GNU
+named tied operands such as `"[sum]"`, which the parser's x86 constraint gate
+had incorrectly rejected.
+
+The patch now:
+
+1. recognizes the relevant constant builtin and conditional-expression shapes
+   during asm constness classification;
+2. defers only immediate-operand constness checks in the normal code-generation
+   path until optimized IR, then rejects any immediate-only input still
+   represented by a runtime value (preventing the backend's zero placeholder
+   from becoming a silent miscompile);
+3. leaves `-fsyntax-only` on its parser-time diagnostics path;
+4. accepts GNU named matching constraints; and
+5. covers valid constant/local/always-inline operands and dynamic operands in
+   `tests/regression/check_asm_operand_diagnostics.sh`.
+
+Validation on the rebased tree:
+
+- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`: passed during the fast CI run.
+- LCCC fastbuild: passed (`-O1`, two Cargo jobs).
+- `CCC=.../target/fastbuild/lccc GCC=gcc bash
+  tests/regression/check_asm_operand_diagnostics.sh`: passed.
+- Focused local `__auto_type` reproducer: constant value emitted as `.long 17`;
+  dynamic input rejected after optimization.
+- Kernel objects `init/main.o` and `arch/x86/lib/csum-wrappers_64.o`: compiled
+  with LCCC after the corresponding fixes. Real objtool completed those object
+  checks with warnings, not errors.
+
+## Linux 6.18.58 build / boot results
+
+Source and patches were taken from the `ms178/archpkgbuilds` package at commit
+`29bec59d` (`_major=6.18`, `_minor=58`); the package carries 29 custom patches.
+The checked tree was re-extracted and prepared with `CC=lccc`, `LD=lccc-ld`,
+and `HOSTCC=gcc` (host tools only).
+
+On the rebased LCCC build:
+
+- Linux `make prepare` completed.
+- Boot-code build passed the required 32 KiB setup limit. The rebased boot-only
+  run measured `_end=26496` bytes (6272 bytes headroom); flat setup output was
+  byte-identical to the GNU ld oracle, and the ISA scan found no BMI/ABM/VEX/
+  SSE4.2-class instructions in 24 objects.
+- Full `bzImage` and `vmlinux` build passed with LCCC + lccc-ld in 1237 seconds.
+  The kernel VM build measured setup `_end=28000` bytes (4768 bytes headroom);
+  its ISA scan passed (43 objects, no post-baseline instruction-class escapes).
+- QEMU TCG boot passed all serial checks: LCCC kernel banner, SCHED_BORE,
+  SCHED_CACHE/CACHE_HOT_BUDDY, 800 Hz, CACHY, BBR, PREEMPT, SMP/two CPUs, BORE
+  per-task score, both serial sentinels, and clean completion/poweroff. Full log:
+  `/home/user/artifacts/qemu-boot-rebased-6.18.58.log`.
+- Build log:
+  `/home/user/artifacts/linux-6.18.58-rebased-vm-build.log`.
+
+**Configuration scope:** this successful full build/boot used the repository's
+QEMU-focused `allnoconfig + scripts/kernel-vm.fragment`, not the upstream x86
+`defconfig`/package default. It verifies a complete, bootable, minimal
+CachyMod-feature kernel; it does not close the separate default-defconfig
+build requirement. SCHED_CLASS_EXT/BTF enablement and full package-config
+coverage were not demonstrated by this VM config.
+
+## `ci_local.sh --fast` attempt and limits
+
+The fast CI run was attempted after rebasing. It was not a green run and was
+stopped during Clippy after the root filesystem reached 100% usage. Cleanup of
+large Cargo incremental outputs restored 5.3 GiB free; the LCCC fastbuild binary
+and `/home/user/ms178-1.patch` were retained.
+
+Environment/test blockers observed before stopping:
+
+- The host has GCC 14.2, not the pinned GCC 16.2 oracle. Passing
+  `GCC16=gcc` let some checks run, but made version-sensitive differential
+  checks compare against the wrong compiler; related oracle gates correctly
+  failed or skipped. Do not treat those mismatches as resolved compiler bugs
+  without rerunning with the pinned oracle.
+- No GCC 16 executable, Clang, i386 libc headers/multilib, or i386 `libgcc` was
+  available. Some 32-bit tests failed to build for that reason.
+- Clippy itself was terminated when its compilation filled the filesystem.
+  Rustfmt and the MSRV check did pass before that point.
+
+The CI attempt therefore supplies useful partial evidence, but is not a
+`--fast` pass. Re-run it on a workspace with the required pinned oracle/tooling
+and enough disk for the Clippy target.
+
+## Remaining work
+
+1. Run `ci_local.sh --fast` to completion with GCC 16.2, Clang, and the i386
+   toolchain installed; investigate any failures that remain with those
+   prerequisites rather than the GCC 14 fallback.
+2. Build the package/default `defconfig` kernel with LCCC/lccc-ld (the successful
+   full build above used the dedicated VM config), then attempt a QEMU boot of
+   that config if the initramfs/device support is available.
+3. Determine whether to enable and validate SCHED_CLASS_EXT/BTF in the VM
+   configuration, then expand QEMU checks to that feature if feasible.
+4. Continue the requested GCC/Clang/ICX/ICC workload benchmarking (benchmark
+   corpus, zlib-ng/gzip, Expat) using reproducible repeated timings and
+   transparent VM/PMU limitations; no new benchmark comparison was completed
+   in this kernel-focused session.
